  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x+1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
